  "sourceFindings": [
    "Prayer 454–468 adds fifteen complete selected numbered reports, thirty-nine English segments and nine complete digital chapters (79–87). All fifteen Arabic paragraphs and all prior 453 entry objects remain unchanged. Full chains, repeated calls, narrator uncertainty and transmission notes remain in source order.",
    "Historical print PDF pages 128–132 and modern print PDF pages 124–128 were visually inspected. Modern labels 466–482 were matched individually: the material of 478–479 and 480 remains one selected entry at 466. Pinned English references 1.8.455–1.8.469 each retain their own source/record hashes. Bewley 459 is explicitly marked abbreviated, with its pointer to 445 disclosed.",
    "454 preserves the selected Most High honorific and both consecutive from links in the chain. 455 retains the full hearing link and small-door wording. 456 preserves both opening transmitters, singular/plural wording and the forgotten final question. 457 does not import the longer parallel’s release and testimony. 458 retains the who/where uncertainty. 459 retains both calls to Ka‘b and all four honorifics.",
    "460 and 461 retain their different grammatical persons and full closing transmissions; no at night phrase is inserted from the prints into 460. 462 retains the selected shorter setting and provisional literal shy wording without interpretive parentheses. 463 preserves the closing Ibn Shihab / Sa‘id chain. 464 retains remembered knowledge of the parents without importing a puberty claim. 467 preserves the selected Abu Burdah name while disclosing the historical print’s Burayd variant.",
    "465 retains the embedded meaning, for him clarification, ablution done well, twenty-five degrees and forgiveness before mercy. Both inspected prints include a causing-harm clause absent from the selected source. Al-Ayni explicitly records copies omitting it, so the selected omission is documented as a variant rather than silently repaired.",
    "466 preserves the Ibn ‘Umar / Ibn ‘Amr uncertainty and complete memory-correction transmission. The final بهذا is a separate literal transmission note, With this, supported as an abbreviated pointer by the consulted commentary. Longer parallel wording is not imported. 468 retains the forgotten prayer name, all movements, four takbirs, both or longer alternatives and the final indirect I was informed attribution. Its afternoon wording follows the selected العشي and modern print; the conflicting historical/English night reading and Ibn Hajar’s discussion remain explicit.",
    "Three original Arabic chapter introductions (80, 85, 86) remain separately browseable and saved offline. Their English translation is pending; none enters a numbered daily session or full narration script. All seven other active book files, existing recordings and artwork remain unchanged."
  ],
